{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>These are the words.</b> Everywhere 'these' is said, it invalidates the preceding ones, for the preceding ones are commandments and judgments, whereas these are mostly words of rebuke and instruction. And one should not object from 'these are the commandments and the statutes and the judgments' written just before it and its like, because there the matter concludes." "<b>To all Israel.</b> This teaches that Moses' voice went the distance of the camp of Israel." "<b>Across the Jordan.</b> And which side? That side which is across in the land of Moab, and not that side which is in the land of Canaan." "<b>In the wilderness, in the Arabah.</b> That is, these words that Moses spoke across the Jordan — Moses had already spoken them to them in the places that he specifies, and a similar pattern we find in the pericashah of Va'etchanan, concerning 'across the Jordan, in the valley opposite Beth-peor,' etc. <b>In the wilderness</b> — opposite Mount Sinai, which is called the wilderness of Sinai." "<b>In the Arabah.</b> In another place, in that very wilderness which is called Arabah, and so it says in this pericashah: 'and we passed,' etc., 'by way of the Arabah, from Elath and from Ezion-geber,' etc. And which place in it? That which is opposite the Sea of Reeds — it is the great and awesome wilderness of Paran, which begins from Etham, as it is written, 'and they encamped at Etham, which is at the edge of the wilderness,' and it comes and reaches as far as the land of Moab, as it is written, 'and we passed through the wilderness of Moab.' And thus in the pericashah of Ekev: 'who led you, HaShem your Elohim, these forty years in the wilderness' — and this wilderness is the one about which Rashi commented in the pericashah of Masei, that Ezekiel called it 'the wilderness of the peoples,' because many inhabited places are alongside it. And it is the wilderness of Shur, it is the wilderness of Etham, it is the wilderness of Sin, it is the wilderness of Paran, it is the wilderness of Sinai, it is the wilderness of Kadesh, it is the wilderness of Zin, it is the wilderness of Kedemoth, it is the wilderness of Moab — for after the places alongside it they call it 'such-and-such wilderness' and 'such-and-such wilderness.'" "<b>Between Paran.</b> Since it is a great wilderness, it gives you a sign where he is in it." "<b>And between Tofel and Lavan.</b> We may say, by force of the straightforward reasoning, that these are places, and likewise ודי זהב, but they are not mentioned in the chapter of the journeys, or they were mentioned in the chapter of the journeys under other names, such as Ritmah and Divon Gad and Almon Divateimah, and Snir, which is called by four names." "<b>And Hatzerot.</b> Rashi [explained] in the dispute of Korah. And if you ask: according to the order of Scripture, the dispute of Korah was in the wilderness of Paran after the sending of the spies; and further, how did Onkelos translate 'ובחצירות דארגיזו על בשרא' — was it not at the Graves of Craving that they provoked, and it is written 'they journeyed from the Graves of Craving and encamped at Hatzerot'? One may answer: had Rashi stated that the dispute of Korah was in the wilderness of Paran, we would not know where it was, since it is a great wilderness, a journey of many days, as we said above; so he adduced Hatzerot, which is an identifiable place known to them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:51:24.488065811Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}